adjudication.rationale:
In apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts the secret check is:

```ts
if (req.headers[process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME || "calcom-webhook-secret"] !== process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET) {
  return res.status(403)
}
```

If `process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` is unset (i.e., `undefined`), and the request omits the webhook header, then `req.headers["calcom-webhook-secret"]` is also `undefined`. The comparison becomes `undefined !== undefined` which is false, so the request is treated as authenticated and proceeds. The handler then allows updating/creating credentials for any `userId` that exists, so this is a real authentication bypass when the secret is not configured.

The new API route decrypts and parses `keys` without any error handling:

```ts
const keys = JSON.parse(
  symmetricDecrypt(reqBody.keys, process.env.CALCOM_APP_CREDENTIAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY || "")
);
```

If `CALCOM_APP_CREDENTIAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY` is missing (falls back to `""`), wrong length, or the ciphertext is invalid, `symmetricDecrypt(...)` can throw or return non-JSON plaintext, and `JSON.parse(...)` can throw. There is no try/catch around this block (nor a Next.js error handler), so the request would result in an unhandled exception and a 500 response instead of a controlled 4xx error. This behavior is directly verifiable from the diff and the `|| ""` fallback makes the failure mode more likely.
